    "Escape LI Text": [
      {
        "json": {
          "commentary_escaped": "Your ISR is faster than your CAN hardware—and it’s deleting your data.\n\nA shipped automotive ECU intermittently dropped CAN frames in the field for two months. Telemetry showed sporadic checksum mismatches and missing sequence IDs under burst traffic at 500 kbps, especially after wake-from-sleep. Unit tests and HIL passed, but long-haul road logs revealed silent data corruption and occasional watchdog nudges with no reproducible trigger in the lab.\n\nThe RX interrupt handler cleared the receive flag as its first instruction, before the CAN peripheral had completed latching the frame into the mailbox. Under certain timing \\(bus-heavy ISR nesting, clock drift after sleep, and DMA contention\\), the clear acknowledged the interrupt source early, the hardware overwrote the RX buffer, and the driver then read either stale or partially updated data. The race hid in tests because simulated traffic didn’t align the ISR entry with the peripheral’s write window.\n\nWe reordered the ISR to read and validate the mailbox first, then clear the flag after data is safely copied to a software queue, adding a memory barrier to enforce ordering. We disabled preemption during the critical read, added double-read consistency checks on DLC/ID, and introduced a hardware-ready poll with a bounded timeout. Regression now includes stress patterns that sweep ISR latency vs. peripheral write timing, plus fault-injection to jitter clocks and enable nested interrupts. Takeaway: in interrupt-driven drivers, never acknowledge before you own the data—tie flag clearing to a verified, ordered read path and test across timing phase space, not just function paths.\n\n\\#Automotive \\#Embedded \\#Debugging \\#DataIntegrity \\#EmbeddedSystems \\#FutureOfWork \\#DigitalTransformation"
        }
      }
    ]
  },
